ading on one line, the recipients in one sideways-scrolling line of 44 px chips, the message sized to its text (`field-sizing: content`) from two rows to `max-h-32`, and the mode beside Send, with one notice slot (`composerLine`) as a sideways-scrolling line between them; under `short` (`max-height: 500px`) the heading shares a line with the recipients, the message, held at two rows, shares one with the mode and Send, and the notice slot is the third and last row. The slot holds one notice at a time, highest first: the recipient limit, then a refused send (`Could not send:`), then the Excluded line. A higher notice hiding the Excluded line hides no name, because every excluded session's chip carries its reason (`<label> · <reason>`). The message's `min-h-16` carries the important modifier because the unlayered below-1280 touch floor (`textarea { min-height: 44px }`) otherwise wins over a utility and the box shrank on its first character. The e2e holds it to 45% of the viewport with a filled message at 390x664, 640x664, 700x664, 844x390 and 667x375 (43.1% at the portrait sizes). Its count reads `recipients.length` and `selected.size`, never the chips on screen, so a scrolled-away chip is still counted and reachable. Selection is what the human ticked, not what the filters currently show, so narrowing after ticking never silently drops a recipient, and the composer names every selected session. Three decisions follow from that. Selection, filters and the draft (message and mode) are page state by design: they live while `/agents` is mounted and reset when it unmounts; the draft is held by the page rather than the composer, so clearing the selection and picking again keeps the typed message and its mode, a refused send keeps them beside its `Could not send:` line, and a successful send leaves the page for the broadcast view, so the next visit starts empty. Wire order is tick order: `session_ids` go out in the order the sessions were selected, and the server keeps that order through deduplication and targeting (`broadcasts.go`). Exclusion is one-directional in two senses. Between selection and send: a selected session that has left the registry, or does not advertise the chosen mode, drops out of the send but never out of the selection, so when it rejoins (or the mode changes) it re-enters the send by itself, and nothing removes a session from the selection that the human did not untick. Between the SPA and the server: the SPA's exclusion is final, since an excluded session is not in `session_ids` at all, and the server can only narrow the set it is given - it re-judges each requested session against one registry read (`broadcasts.go`) and may exclude more, never add. The composer refuses a send over `MAX_BROADCAST_RECIPIENTS` (`@legion/contracts`, generated into Go as `contracts.MaxBroadcastRecipients`, the server's own limit) before asking: Send is disabled and a line names the limit and the count. `broadcastPlan` decides, from the live capability list, which of them the chosen mode reaches: one that does not advertise it is shown excluded and left out, never switched to another mode, which is exactly what the server repeats against its own registry read. Sending `POST /api/v1/broadcasts` navigates to `/agents/broadcasts/<id>` (`BroadcastPage.tsx`) carrying the response's `excluded` in router state, since exclusions are a fact about one send and the server stores none. That page lists every recipient's delivery state and reply through the same `DeliveryStatus` an agent card uses, reads a recipient with no attempt as `Not sent` rather than as delivered (delivery runs behind the send), and offers each unsettled recipient the agent card's `DeliveryRetry`; `/agents/broadcasts` (`BroadcastsPage.tsx`) keeps earlier sends reachable. Both are keyed under `["broadcast", …]`, the prefix `api/sse.ts` invalidates on every issue-less message event, so a recipient's receipt or answer lands without a poll. A session's replies to the viewer's direct messages count unread (`features/agents/unread.ts`, from `GET /me/agents/state`) on the Agents navigation badge and the agent's row until the viewer opens the row or the live view (`features/agent-view/`, `/agents/<id>/live`), which marks read through the newest reply on screen (a failed mark is retried, and `user_agent_state.updated` for the viewer's login refreshes the count on their other tabs and devices); opening a row shows every exchange the server flagged `unread` when it opened (`useUnreadAtOpen` freezes that set, so marking those replies read never folds them away; the SPA renders the server's verdict and derives none of its own), and a session that has ended keeps a `Replied, no longer connected` row (`EndedAgentsWithReplies.tsx`) whose Open reads it. The live view interleaves the stored messages with the relayed stream: the session's replies as `Reply via Dispatch`, the viewer's own messages on the right, and anything anyone else sent the session with its author (and issue key) on the session's side.
